title Caravan serve -- one virtualiser serves two clients, and neither hears the other's bell
prompt Autonomous loop -- read Kyri and Radiant Style, then REMEMBER, the itineraries, and the newest log recommends; close open agent-closable reds first, then take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; send each finished increment as its own signed round.
think Read context/KYRI.md and context/RADIANT_STYLE.md first, so the voice shaped the lap from the first token; then crux/REMEMBER.md, the double-seat itinerary's Microkernel Target section, and the top rows of session-logs/README.md.
think Standfast first -- crux/REDS.md read whole. Every living row is closed and released (80, 83, 84, 85 including the 20260819.134229 reopen-and-close), and both prevention looms run GREEN on metal this lap: witness_claim_check_witness and living_card_ascii_witness. No open agent-closable red stands, so constructive work may begin.
think The running thread is unambiguous. The 20260819.143111 recommend named the next crux by name -- the third party: one virtualiser serving two clients in one run, each answered on its own channel with neither able to hear the other's bell, where the shape stops being a pair and becomes a system.
think Council rota deep-read this lap: foundations/20260702-165412_the-happy-zone-and-the-thin-edge.md. Its teaching lands squarely here -- two small answers at every seam, the multiplication of wholes collapsed into an addition of parts. The serve ring is exactly that: the seam between one server and two clients checked by the refusals on both sides rather than by one large integrated run.
think Named the module serve.rye under the Comlink tendency -- the clearest warm word for what it does, no forced coinage.
think Wrote caravan/systems/serial_two_clients.bron first, since the declaration is where isolation belongs. Both clients get the duplex shape (rx_X the virtualiser writes and the client reads, tx_X the client writes and the virtualiser reads); no channel joins the clients and neither holds a grant on the other's memory, so isolation is what the document SAYS rather than a habit the code keeps. It read whole on the first pass -- domains 3, channels 2, regions 4, grants 8.
think Reused roundtrip's directed bells and four-field plan wire rather than re-growing them, since the per-pair direction is exactly the property the third party tests. What serve adds of its own is the addressed message: every ask reads `serve: ask for <client>` and every answer `serve: answer from <client>`, each derived from what the child already knows -- the requester from the peer it serves, the replier from its own domain label.
think Ordered the nine-step plan so the crux is step two. The virtualiser rings client_b and nobody else; client_a then collects and answers Unheard. Steps six through nine serve client_a in the same run and collect from client_b a second time, proving the newer conversation left the older one standing.
obs GREEN on the first metal pass, walls and trip together. Then both RED paths proven before the green was trusted, each on a temporary probe copy that was removed after: naming a bell by its sender alone let client_a hear the ring meant for client_b and walk on into the regions, reporting `bytes differed` where `Unheard` belonged -- one server broadcasting to every client it serves, which is precisely what a per-pair direction prevents. Addressing every ask to a fixed client made client_a read its neighbor's question and decline to answer it.
obs The addressed message is a second wall on the same seam, and it is the one that survives a bell going wrong. A ring can only say something happened; the bytes say for whom. Two small answers at the seam, exactly as the rota doc asks.
obs Isolation between clients now stops being an absence of traffic and becomes a refusal a witness watches happen -- the difference between a server that has no second client to leak to and one that would refuse if it did.
obs The witness measures from outside the program too: four directed bells each rung exactly once, and each client's answer standing in its own region signed by the client that wrote it.
obs One correction taken in-lap: the region stores are `.bin`, never `.store` -- the first witness draft guessed the extension and its own assert caught it before any claim was made. Measurement beats memory, at the smallest possible scale.
loom witness=caravan_serve_witness result=green steps=9 walls=3 bells=4 rings_each=1 regions=4 declared_bytes=16384
file caravan/systems/serial_two_clients.bron the declaration where isolation lives -- both clients duplex, no channel or grant joining them
file caravan/serve.rye the serve ring -- addressed messages, per-client walls, and the nine-step served run
file tools/caravan_serve_witness.rish the witness -- the crux Unheard, the three walls, four bells measured from outside, each answer signed by its own client
file caravan/README.md the module row and the "Why One Server Serves Two Clients" section, synced in the same commit
file crux/REMEMBER.md live edge condensed to twelve rings and refilled with the next crux; git nib pinned
recommend keep-going -- the arc is a system now rather than a pair. Next crux: either the unprompted ask (a client ringing a server that has not spoken first, which inverts who starts the conversation) or restart-under-load, where a served child falls mid-conversation and the supervisor's three exit meanings meet a standing channel. No key, no funds, no gate.
